<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raphael</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Raffaello Sanzio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raphael (Raffaello Sanzio) was an Italian painter and architect of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, born in Urbino in 1483. After work in Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1508, where he painted the frescoes of the Vatican Stanze, including The School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, for Popes Julius II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a>. He also succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a> as architect of Saint Peter's Basilica. He died in Rome aged 37.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raphael.jpg" alt="Raphael" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raffaello_Sanzio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raphael was born in Urbino, a small city of the Papal States whose court had been established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a>, created Duke of Urbino by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>. His father, Giovanni Santi, was court painter to the Duke and also a poet who wrote a rhymed chronicle of Federico's life. Raphael's mother Màgia died in 1491, and his father on 1 August 1494, so that he was an orphan at eleven. His guardian became his uncle Bartolomeo, a priest, who engaged in litigation with his stepmother. Raphael probably took part in managing his father's workshop from a very early age.</p><p>He probably trained in the workshop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perugino">Pietro Perugino</a> and was described as a fully trained master by 1500. From about 1504 he spent a good deal of time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, though possibly never as a continuous resident. A letter of recommendation of October 1504 describes him as a young painter who had decided to study there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1508 Raphael moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he lived for the rest of his life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> invited him, perhaps at the suggestion of the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Donato Bramante</a>, who came from just outside Urbino and was distantly related to him. Julius at once commissioned him to fresco what was meant to become the Pope's private library in the Vatican Palace, a far larger commission than any he had had before. The Raphael Rooms, with The School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the Stanza della Segnatura, became the largest work of his career.</p><p>According to Vasari, Raphael eventually had a workshop of fifty pupils and assistants, arguably the largest team under any single old master painter. The most important were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-romano">Giulio Romano</a> and Gianfrancesco Penni, who were left many of his drawings and continued the workshop after his death. In 1514 he became engaged to Maria Bibbiena, Cardinal Bibbiena's niece, but the marriage had not taken place when she died in 1520.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Raphael was one of the finest draftsmen in the history of Western art and used drawings extensively to plan his compositions; over four hundred sheets survive. He made no prints himself but collaborated with Marcantonio Raimondi on about fifty engravings, which were the main way his art was experienced outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> until the twentieth century. His influence in his own century was less than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, but those who disliked the excesses of Mannerism soon held him up as the ideal balanced painter. In the 18th and 19th centuries his serene and harmonious qualities were again regarded as the highest models.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raphael” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their collections, built up by Paul III and his descendants in Rome and Parma, included works by Titian, <b>Raphael</b> and Correggio and the classical sculptures of the Palazzo Farnese in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Augustus the Strong and Augustus III Dresden became one of the finest Baroque cities in Europe, with the Zwinger, the Frauenkirche, the Green Vault treasury and a picture gallery that acquired <b>Raphael</b>’s Sistine Madonna in 1754.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…IV built the Sistine Chapel and founded the Vatican Library, Julius II commissioned Michelangelo’s ceiling of the Sistine Chapel and <b>Raphael</b>’s frescoes in the Vatican, and the new basilica of Saint Peter, begun in 1506, was completed by Carlo Maderno and Gian Lorenzo Bernini in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The painters <b>Raphael</b> and Annibale Carracci, the composer Arcangelo Corelli and the architect Baldassare Peruzzi are buried there, and a 16th-century confraternity of artists and musicians, later joined by Bernini, Cortona and Algardi, kept up worship in one chapel; it survives as the Pontifical Academy of Fine Arts.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish singer <b>Raphael</b> recorded it in the mid-1960s, the Azerbaijani singer Rashid Beibutov in the 1960s, and Joan Baez on her album Gracias a la Vida in 1974; later versions include those of Eugenia León, Lila Downs (1999), Natalia Lafourcade and Ángela Aguilar (both 2018), and Dulce…</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-llorona">La Llorona</a></li><li><blockquote>Brian <b>Raphael</b> Nabors arranged it for string quartet in 2018.</blockquote><a href="/songs/linstead-market">Linstead Market</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Urbino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.73,12.64&amp;year=1507" aria-label="Urbino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 614 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raphael"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 884h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.73,12.64&amp;year=1507">What was here in 1507? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
